          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01XehASofYBqHFodNNkHrZ16",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022concepts/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point\\ndescription: Centralizing configuration resolution to prevent scattering and enable auditability.\\ntimestamp: 2025-01-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - pattern/Configuration-Safety\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both source pages explicitly state centralizing strategy; the pattern across them is clear and intentional.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2025-07-14\\n---\\n\\n**Single Composition Point** is an architectural principle that resolves sensitive or location-critical configuration in one place rather than scattering it across the codebase or across process boundaries. This prevents both credential leakage and path-discovery logic from being duplicated or bypassed.\\n\\nTwo design decisions in Grimoire implement this principle:\\n\\n**[[credential-scoping]]** centralizes where the Anthropic API key enters the system. A secrets loader reads the key once at startup and injects it directly into the child Ingest (or Query) agent\u0027s process environment \u2014 never into the Hub\u0027s own environment. This single point of injection prevents the Hub itself from ever holding or leaking the credential.\\n\\n**[[runtime-paths]]** centralizes where all file-system locations are resolved. A single path resolver reads configuration from all sources (CLI, environment, config file, defaults) in one place at startup, validates every location exists, and provides pre-resolved paths to all downstream code. No other part of the codebase reads the working directory or rediscovers paths on its own.\\n\\nBoth enforce their decisions upstream: the Hub cannot mutate its environment (so credential scoping is enforced by design), and downstream code cannot read the working directory (so path logic is enforced through the API). Because configuration flows through these single points, they become auditable choke points \u2014 you can reason about how credentials move or where files come from by examining one place rather than hunting through the codebase.\\n\u0022}"
        }
