      "assistant_text": "## Answer\n\nYour credential-scoping and runtime-path decisions embody the same architectural principle: **centralizing configuration resolution to prevent it from scattering across the codebase and to enable auditability**.\n\n**[[credential-scoping]]** centralizes where the Anthropic API key enters the system: a secrets loader reads it once at startup and injects it directly into the child Ingest or Query agent\u0027s process environment, never into the Hub\u0027s own environment. This single point of injection is enforced by design \u2014 the Hub cannot mutate its own environment, so the credential has nowhere to leak to.\n\n**[[runtime-paths]]** centralizes where all file-system locations are resolved: a single path resolver reads configuration from all sources (CLI, environment, config file, defaults) at startup, validates every location exists, and provides pre-resolved paths to all downstream code. No other part of the codebase reads the working directory or rediscovers paths on its own.\n\nBoth enforce their decisions upstream: downstream code cannot bypass the controlled path. Because configuration flows through these single composition points, they become auditable choke points \u2014 you can reason about credential flow or file access from examining one place rather than hunting through the codebase.\n\nI\u0027ve saved this connection as a new page, [[concepts/single-composition-point]].",
